## The change

Style and markup only; no new image file.

- `apps/storytime/app/[locale]/st/page.tsx`: the root `main` carries `data-place` and `data-hero`
  from `placeFor(null)` (`meadow` / `path`), and the page renders the shared `Frame` with
  `variant="cover"`, the fallback heading as the plaque title, and `bar="Storytime Advantage"` as
  the label. The heading moves to the plaque; the instruction stays in a cream card; the teacher
  link and the sign-out form stay.
- `apps/storytime/app/[locale]/frame.tsx`: `FrameProps` becomes a union of the route props
  (`level` and `week`) and the bar props (`bar`). A week page passes the route numbers as before;
  the signed-in home passes `bar`. The rendered markup is unchanged for the week pages.
- `apps/storytime/__tests__/phase3-browser-gate.sh`: 3 new Playwright rows. Signed in, `/th/st`
  must paint `main[data-place]::before` with a `url(...)` computed background-image at 390 x 844,
  980 x 800, and 1280 x 800. The rows write viewport screenshots under `~/.cache/st-home-look/`.
  The script's row count goes from 81 to 84.

No first-load growth: the frame logo and the two place pictures are the shipped look assets, and
the gate reads 284267 bytes without the two place pictures. The plan's last recorded value was
284270 bytes at `3804ab55a`, so the change is inside run variance.

## Environment note (not a code change)

This fresh worktree linked `node_modules` and `apps/storytime/node_modules` to the main checkout.
Turbopack refused a build over that link (`Symlink ... could not be resolved`), and the first gate
builds failed before they tested the code. The worktree then ran
`pnpm install --offline --frozen-lockfile` and
`pnpm turbo run build --filter=storytime^... --concurrency=1` (all 6 packages cached). Both write
only gitignored paths. No lockfile, no `next-env.d.ts`, and no app file changed for this step.

The Next.js 16.4.1 build stopped once with the `SecurityFatalError` upgrade notice after a
successful compile and page generation. The plan retry rule F4 allows one rerun; the rerun read the
receipt and exited 0 with `PASS: Production build exits 0`. No `next upgrade` ran.

